\ The network card misbehaving (V4): what the proofs assume the driver survives.
\ Each fault is made in one line, so the network task cannot run in between.
: used-entry  ( vq k -- addr )  over vq-size 1- and 8 * 4 +  swap vq-used + ;
: forge  ( id len -- )  \ the device hands back a chain it was never given
  rx-vq dup vq-seen @ used-entry  tuck 4 + l!  l!
  rx-vq vq-seen @ 1+ $ffff and  rx-vq vq-used 2 + w! ;
\ A buffer id the driver never posted: ignored, nothing copied.
pad 64 $aa fill  999 64 forge  pad net-poll .  pad c@ .
\ Wild ids with absurd lengths, five in turn: each ignored, and none left over.
: burst  ( -- )  5 0 do  65535 i - $7fffffff forge  pad net-poll .  loop ;
burst
rx-vq vq-used? .
\ A device that never completes a request: the wait gives up and throws.
8 new-vq constant dead-vq
: wait-dead  ( -- )  dead-vq vq-wait ;
' wait-dead catch .
\ A caller asking to send more than the buffer holds, or a negative length:
\ the copy stops at the buffer's end, so the MAC address after it is intact.
\ An empty frame is not sent: QEMU's card stops working if given one.
: mac.  ( -- )  6 0 do  net-mac i + c@ .  loop ;
mac.
pad 5000 net-send  mac.
pad -1 net-send  mac.
pad 0 net-send  pad 60 net-send  .( still sending)
\ And the system carries on.
2 3 + .
net-up? 0<> .
